content.text:
Because of the direction of your project references. `LivingEntity` lives in `Engine.Models`, `SkillFactory` lives in `Engine.Services`, and only Services references Models, not the other way round:

```
WPFUI → Engine.ViewModels → Engine.Services → Engine.Models → Engine.Core
```

A project can only see the projects it references, so Models cannot see anything in Services. You also can't add the reference the other way, because Services already references Models and .NET rejects circular project references.

This is intended, and the rest of your code already follows it: no model calls `ItemFactory` or `RecipeFactory` either. The model only receives the finished object, and the caller one layer up fetches it from the factory.

For skills, do the same as you did for recipes:

```csharp
// Engine.Models – LivingEntity or Player: only takes a finished Skill
public void LearnSkill(Skill skill)
{
    if(!Skills.Any(s => s.ID == skill.ID))
    {
        Skills.Add(skill);
    }
}
```

```csharp
// Engine.ViewModels – e.g. CharacterCreationViewModel.GetPlayer(), next to LearnRecipe
player.LearnSkill(SkillFactory.SkillByID(1));
```

The same applies when loading a save: `SaveGameService` reads the skill IDs and calls the factory, as `PopulatePlayerRecipes` does for recipes.

Two small things I noticed while looking:

- **Missing namespace:** [SkilllFactory.cs](Engine.Services/Factories/SkilllFactory.cs) has no `namespace Engine.Services.Factories;` line, so the class sits in the global namespace, unlike your other factories.
- **`Skills` is never initialised:** [LivingEntity.cs:24](Engine.Models/LivingEntity.cs#L24) declares `List<Skill> Skills` without creating the list, so the first `Skills.Add` will throw a `NullReferenceException`. Use an `ObservableCollection<Skill>` with `= new()` like `Recipes` in `Player`, so the UI can bind to it later.

